# Numbering sources

Each numbering source is a separate process. SipOut, Runexis, UIS, Aurora Telecom, MTS Exolve, Voximplant, MCN Telecom, and Finenumbers are the sources in this release. SipOut reads stay in the SipOut rule. Runexis reads stay in the Runexis rule. UIS reads stay in the UIS rule. Aurora Telecom reads stay in the Aurora Telecom rule. MTS Exolve reads stay in the MTS Exolve rule. Voximplant reads stay in the Voximplant rule. MCN Telecom reads stay in the MCN Telecom rule. Finenumbers reads stay in the Finenumbers rule. Do not invent a source, a host, or a method.

SipOut owns `numbering_sync_state` and `numbering_sync_stage`. Runexis owns `runexis_sync_state` and `runexis_sync_stage`. UIS owns `uis_sync_state` and `uis_sync_stage`. Aurora Telecom owns `aurora_sync_state`, `aurora_sync_stage`, and `aurora_files`. MTS Exolve owns `exolve_sync_state` and `exolve_sync_stage`. Voximplant owns `voximplant_sync_state` and `voximplant_sync_stage`. MCN Telecom owns `mcn_sync_state` and `mcn_sync_stage`. Finenumbers owns `finenumbers_sync_state` and `finenumbers_sync_stage`. Each state row stays `id = 1` with `CHECK (id = 1)`. A source does not insert into the other source's tables and does not drop the other check. Each source has its own module, its own `globalThis` slot, and its own text log. Finenumbers uses the existing PSTN reader and does not add a vendor client.

`free_numbers` and `purchased_numbers` are the shared catalogs. A publish deletes and inserts only the rows of that source's provider literal, in one transaction. SipOut, Runexis, UIS, MTS Exolve, Voximplant, and MCN Telecom each replace only the catalog side that load read: purchased or free. The other side stays. A number may sit in both catalogs. Aurora Telecom replaces its free rows and clears its purchased rows. Finenumbers replaces its rows in both catalogs. The Aurora Telecom provider literal is `Aurora Telecom`. The MTS Exolve provider literal is `MTS Exolve`. The Voximplant provider literal is `Voximplant`. The MCN Telecom provider literal is `MCN Telecom`. The Finenumbers provider literal is `Finenumbers`. A purchased load does not read free numbers. A free load does not read purchased numbers. In the chain, a failed purchased stage does not start that source's free stage. Aurora Telecom reads the saved http or https links. A successful Aurora Telecom load writes the free rows and clears the purchased Aurora Telecom rows. An empty Aurora Telecom set does not publish. Both HTTP lists must succeed before either catalog is replaced. A failed read leaves the catalogs. The provider name is a literal in the SQL. Do not accept a source name from a request, a flag, or a setting.

The SipOut walk, the SipOut access check, and the SipOut key save share one in-memory holder. The key save does not take the database lease. Do not split that holder. The Runexis walk, the Runexis access check, and the Runexis password save share a different holder. The UIS walk, the UIS access check, and the UIS key save share a third holder. The Aurora Telecom link save, the link check, and the walk share a fourth holder. The link save and the link check do not take the database lease. The MTS Exolve walk, the MTS Exolve access check, and the MTS Exolve key save share a fifth holder. The key save does not take the database lease. The Voximplant walk, the Voximplant access check, and the Voximplant key save share a sixth holder. The key save and the access check do not take the database lease. The MCN Telecom walk, the MCN Telecom access check, and the MCN Telecom token save share a seventh holder. The key save and the access check do not take the database lease. The Finenumbers walk is an eighth holder. It has no secret and no access check. `SIGTERM` sets one stop flag. A walk that sees it does not publish. Do not add a second listener for that signal.

Tariff resplit updates ABC, the local number, city, and region on every row of both catalogs. It locks `numbering_sync_state` `id = 1` first, then `runexis_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `uis_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `aurora_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `exolve_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `voximplant_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `mcn_sync_state` `id = 1`, then `finenumbers_sync_state` `id = 1`. A fresh heartbeat on any lease waits. It does not call a numbering source and it does not download Aurora Telecom links. The resplit is batches of 1000, a one-second pause, and a batch transaction of at most 60 seconds. It does not write mask, client, operator, or geography. A tariff file increments `resplit_epoch` and `mask_epoch` in the snapshot transaction. Any error waits 5 seconds and repeats the same id. There is no give-up.

The draft fill calls the shared tariff fragment before the catalog lock. The catalog copy writes the tariff cells, the provider prices, and empty mask, premium, and client cells. It does not call `rtu_mask_lookup`, `cdr_tariff_geo`, or `geography_digit_split`. Every source, including Finenumbers, writes operator and geography as empty. A mask pass and a provider pass fill the tariff cells, the mask, the mask type, and operator and geography. The mask price is written to sale on both catalogs. Provider purchase and subscription stay. Operator and geography come from the stored range snapshot when exactly one range covers the number. The PSTN-range pass fills those two cells the same way. Zero or several ranges leave them. On «Управление РТУ», «Свободные номера», and «Купленные номера», a city of «Мобильные РФ» shows that row's geography in the region cell. The shown region then replaces «Московская область, Город Москва» and «Город Москва, Московская область» with «Московская область», and «Ленинградская область, Город Санкт-Петербург» and «Город Санкт-Петербург, Ленинградская область» with «Ленинградская область». «География» stays unchanged. The stored region stays the tariff region. Decorate does not write region from geography. A failed range read does not clear them and does not download the list again while `loadedAt` is unchanged. The same transaction increments that provider's epoch on `numbering_catalog_state`. Aurora Telecom uses `aurora_epoch` and `aurora_done`. MTS Exolve uses `exolve_epoch` and `exolve_done`. Voximplant uses `voximplant_epoch` and `voximplant_done`. MCN Telecom uses `mcn_epoch` and `mcn_done`. Finenumbers uses `finenumbers_epoch` and `finenumbers_done`. The range snapshot uses `pstn_range_epoch` and `pstn_range_done`. Those counters start at 0. Adding them does not change `decorate_generation`. A mask file increments `mask_epoch` in the snapshot transaction. A phone snapshot increments `client_epoch` only when the set of endpoint number and «Описание» changes, and stores that hash. An unchanged set stores the hash when it is still empty and does not bump. Each bump clears only its own cursor phases. The cursor is the phase, the provider, the last committed id, and the epoch. The next start reads it from the database. The cursor write is in the same transaction as the row update and only when that epoch is unchanged. A mask batch and a provider batch add that batch to the per-provider parsed counters in the same update. Resplit, client, and bootstrap do not. A tariff or mask bump zeros those counters. The provider epoch statement zeros only that provider's counter. Any error waits 5 seconds and repeats the same id. There is no give-up. Done stamps are written only when the epoch is unchanged. The order is resplit, then a full mask pass, then a provider whose epoch is ahead, then the PSTN-range pass when its epoch is ahead, then clients. The range download runs when the next debt is that PSTN pass or the queue is empty. An open provider, mask, or resplit epoch stops the download between pages and stops the catalog walk before the next batch. Neither stop changes the snapshot. After the provider pass the walk starts at the first row. The queue stays busy until every epoch is caught up. A pass sets the matching done stamp only when the epoch is still the value it read. Decorate uses the same lock order, in batches of 1000 with a one-second pause, and a batch transaction of at most 60 seconds. It waits on a fresh heartbeat. The first start still runs one full pass when `decorate_generation` is 0. A future source uses the same fragment, the same copy, and its own epoch literal.

The chain runs one stage at a time: SipOut purchased, SipOut free, Runexis purchased, Runexis free, UIS purchased, UIS free, Aurora Telecom free, MTS Exolve purchased, MTS Exolve free, Voximplant purchased, Voximplant free, MCN Telecom purchased, MCN Telecom free, Finenumbers. On «Синхронизация», «Загрузить купленные», «Загрузить свободные», and «Загрузить все» start immediately. Enter does not start a load. A source without a saved secret skips both of its stages. Finenumbers has no secret and is not skipped for that reason. Secret confirms stay on «Загрузка нумерации». The chain calls the existing start of one stage. It does not add a vendor request. It waits until that source's holder is released and the decorate queue is idle before the next start. There is no extra pause between sources and no chain-wide timeout. «Уже выполняется.» and the vacuum sentence retry the same source. A missing or unreadable secret skips that source. Any other start error moves on. The result is the new `last_finished_at`. A cancel sentence or process stop does not open the tail. Cancel during publishing still masks that source and continues. A second chain does not start. While the chain runs, a card load is refused. Access check and secret save stay on the same holder. The daily run is 17:00 UTC. A process start does not catch a window that has already begun. A chain already running at 17:00 is not queued again. Restart does not resume a chain. Do not add a second `SIGTERM` listener.

Do not write to a numbering-source API unless a task names that exact write. Aurora Telecom may send only the GET named in the Aurora Telecom rule. Do not log a key, a password, a token, a query, a URL, or a CSV body.
